A '''kaas''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''kaas'', "stronghold" or "enclosure") was a type of fortified hilltop garrison constructed by the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]] across the southern regions of [[Southwestern Kaftia]]. The ''kaas'' system formed the military and administrative backbone of the Ufyen state from its origins under the [[Geedi Period]] (~1,000 BCE) through the fall of the [[Warfaa Period]] (~900 AD), and the remains of ''kaas'' forts are among the most visible archaeological features of the region.

== Construction ==

A typical ''kaas'' consisted of a dry-stone perimeter wall enclosing a hilltop or elevated ridge, with an earth rampart on the exterior slope and a cleared field of approach below. The enclosed area varied in size from small signal stations accommodating a dozen soldiers to major garrison forts capable of housing several hundred. Larger ''kaas'' included interior structures for grain storage, water cisterns cut into bedrock, and quarters for the garrison commander.

The choice of site was determined by visibility and control of movement. ''Kaas'' were positioned to overlook river crossings, mountain passes, road junctions, and valley narrows — points where traffic could be observed, taxed, or blocked. Where natural elevation was insufficient, the builders augmented hilltops with stone-faced terracing.

== Function ==

The ''kaas'' served three overlapping functions within the Ufyen state:

=== Military ===
Each ''kaas'' was garrisoned by a detachment of highland soldiers under a commander appointed by the Ufyen court. Garrisons varied in size according to the strategic importance of the position. The forts were spaced at intervals of roughly one to two days' march, enabling a signal and relay system along major routes. During periods of unrest, ''kaas'' garrisons could be reinforced from the highland core or combined into field armies.

=== Administrative ===
''Kaas'' commanders also served as the local representatives of the Ufyen king. They collected tribute from the surrounding population — in the form of grain, livestock, labour, or [[Bir (currency)|bir]] — and transmitted it to the capital at [[Badalga-Jir]] or the customs post at [[Jowhar]]. In the highland provinces, ''kaas''-holding lords formed a military aristocracy that governed the kingdom's territories on behalf of the crown.

=== Commercial ===
Forts positioned along the [[Buran River]] and [[Galbeed River]] corridors functioned as customs posts, levying tariffs on goods moving between the [[Kushish]] highlands and the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast. The ''kaas'' at [[Jowhar]], controlling the point where the Upper Buran flows into the [[Lake Haro]] system, was the most important of these and served as the kingdom's principal revenue collection point.

== History ==

=== Geedi Dynasty ===
The ''kaas'' system originated under the [[Geedi Period]] (~1,000–500 BCE) as a means of securing the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range|mountain passes]] and the iron-producing districts of the [[Kushish]] highlands. During the '''[[Dagaalka Buran]]''' (~700–650 BCE), the network was extended down the full length of the [[Buran River]] to the [[Mesogean Sea]] coast. By the late Geedi period, ''kaas'' forts lined the major routes from [[Lake Haro]] to the coast, forming a chain of fortified positions that sustained the empire's control over its far-flung tributaries.

The maintenance of this extended network — including the provisioning of distant garrisons and the repair of stone fortifications — placed considerable strain on the kingdom's resources and is considered one of the factors contributing to the [[Ancient Southwestern Kaftia#Geedi collapse|Geedi collapse]] (~500 BCE).

=== Dalmar and Warfaa Dynasties ===
The [[Dalmar Period]] (~400–200 BCE) restored and strengthened the ''kaas'' network in the highland core and the Upper Buran valley, but did not attempt to reoccupy the coastal lowland forts abandoned after the Geedi collapse. The Dalmar strategy of controlling trade chokepoints rather than territory depended on a smaller, more concentrated ''kaas'' network focused on the mountain passes and the Jowhar customs post.

The [[Warfaa Period]] (~100 BCE–900 AD) maintained the Dalmar-era network and invested in the fortification of [[Badalga-Jir]] and [[Jowhar]]. The ''kaas'' system remained operational until the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa]] (893–925 AD), when the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] absorbed the Ufyen highlands and the garrisons were abandoned or repurposed.

== Construction labour ==

The construction and maintenance of ''kaas'' forts was carried out primarily through {{W|corvée}} labour. War captives, particularly those taken from [[Bariites|Bariite]] and [[Hiddid]] communities, were employed in quarrying stone and building fortifications. The farming population of the highlands also owed periodic labour service to the ''kaas''-holding lords.

== Archaeological record ==

The remains of ''kaas'' forts are found throughout the [[Kushish]] highlands, the [[Buran River]] valley, and the [[Galbeed River]] corridor. Over 150 sites have been identified through survey and excavation, ranging from small watch-posts to major garrison complexes. The best-preserved examples are in the Upper Buran valley, where the dry climate has preserved stone walls to heights of two to three metres. Pottery, iron tools, and [[Bir (currency)|bir]] currency recovered from ''kaas'' sites have been used to date the construction and occupation phases of the network.

